Each identity is just the definition \(y = \log_a x \Leftrightarrow a^{y} = x\) read in a special case:
\(\log_a 1 = 0\) because \(a^{0} = 1\);
\(\log_a a = 1\) because \(a^{1} = a\);
\(\log_a(a^{x}) = x\): taking the logarithm undoes raising to a power;
\(a^{\log_a x} = x\): raising to a power undoes taking the logarithm.
The last two say that \(a^{x}\) and \(\log_a x\) cancel each other, in either order, which is exactly what inverse functions do.
